Subclinical Iron Deficiency (Non-Anemic)
Undifferentiated or Subclinical Symptom Clusters
Low serum ferritin (<30 µg/L) | Strictly normal Hemoglobin and Hematocrit | Vague fatigue or incidental restless legs syndrome

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: USC-026
Reason for Visit: Acute nocturnal exacerbation of severe leg restlessness and profound exhaustion requiring urgent evaluation to exclude acute venous thromboembolism or severe electrolyte derangement.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated an urgent assessment was required to evaluate severe leg discomfort and sudden inability to weight-bear due to profound fatigue. Immediate clinical examination was mandated to rule out deep vein thrombosis, acute hypokalemia, or impending neurological compromise, preventing severe morbidity prior to the next in-hours period.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I literally cannot keep my legs still, they ache deeply, and I am chewing on ice constantly just to stay awake.
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a three-month history of unexplained fatigue despite adequate rest, culminating in a severe crash tonight. There is a prominent worsening of restless legs syndrome, particularly severe at night, preventing any sleep. The patient describes mild, diffuse hair thinning, brittle nails with subtle longitudinal ridging, and mild glossitis (sore tongue). Additionally, the patient admits to intense pica, notably intense cravings for ice. The patient notes significantly decreased exercise tolerance and difficulty maintaining concentration. They complain of generalized weakness without a focal motor deficit.
Functional Status: Highly functioning at baseline, but currently pacing the room due to severe restless legs.
Social/Safety Concerns: Severe sleep deprivation heavily impacting cognitive safety.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 118/76 mmHg | HR: 88 bpm | Temp: 36.8 C | RR: 16 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 68 Kg
General: Patient appears exhausted, visibly restless, and continuously shifting leg positions. Conjunctivae are non-pale.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular exam reveals a regular rhythm without murmurs. Respiratory exam is clear. Lower extremity evaluation reveals no unilateral swelling or calf tenderness, firmly ruling out deep vein thrombosis. Neurological examination incidentally reveals distal symmetrical paresthesia in a stocking-glove distribution and reduced ankle reflexes bilaterally, suggestive of an early overlapping sensory neuropathy, though motor strength remains 5/5.
Environmental Observations: A large cup of crushed ice is sitting on the bedside table.
Medication Reconciliation: Checked against EHR. Patient occasionally uses over-the-counter magnesium with no relief.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Subclinical Iron Deficiency (Non-Anemic) - Status: Acute exacerbation of secondary restless legs syndrome.
Differentials:

Deep Vein Thrombosis - Ruled out clinically due to the bilateral nature of the symptoms, lack of leg swelling, and absence of localized calf tenderness.

Acute Hypokalemia - Ruled out due to normal muscle power, intact (though reduced) reflexes, and normal regular cardiac rhythm.

Peripheral Arterial Disease - Ruled out due to the presence of strong, bounding dorsalis pedis and posterior tibial pulses bilaterally.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low.

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient required an urgent after-hours assessment due to severe, unrelenting limb discomfort and exhaustion, mimicking acute vascular occlusion or severe electrolyte imbalance. Telephone triage appropriately flagged the necessity to exclude a deep venous thrombosis or hypokalemic paralysis. A rigorous physical examination confirmed normal vascular perfusion, the absence of focal muscular failure, and a classic presentation of restless legs syndrome driven by a systemic deficiency state. This immediate, hands-on clinical evaluation safely ruled out life-threatening vascular pathology, halting an unnecessary emergency department transfer while providing necessary physiological stabilization.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Prescribed a short course of a low-dose dopamine agonist for acute restless leg symptomatic control. The expected investigation results include a serum ferritin below 30 ng/mL with a normal hemoglobin concentration, which will assist in confirming the diagnosis during outpatient pathology follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the severe leg restlessness at night, perform a ten-minute routine of deep calf and hamstring stretches immediately before getting into bed; physically fatiguing the muscle spindles will calm the local nerve firing and allow your central nervous system to initiate sleep.
Home-Based Services: None indicated tonight.
DME/Safety: None required.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 3 days to arrange a comprehensive iron panel, B12, and fasting glucose to investigate the underlying neuropathy.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to the Emergency Department or call 000 if the patient develops unilateral leg swelling, extreme localized calf pain, sudden severe shortness of breath, or complete numbness in the legs.

